    function_tags:['usurper','chaos','fratricide','desert','storm','dismemberment'],
    etymology:'Egyptian Sth or Stš — possibly related to "pillar" or "to dazzle/confuse." The name itself carries ambivalence.',
    primary_sources:['Pyramid Texts (earliest references)','Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride (c.100 CE)','Chester Beatty Papyrus (Contendings of Horus and Set)'],
    key_claims:['Murdered and dismembered Osiris — the cosmic fratricide','Ruled as usurper until Horus restored the rightful order','God of the desert, storms, foreigners, and chaos','Complex figure: also Ra\'s protector against Apophis — demonstrates his ambivalence','In some periods worshipped as sovereign; in others demonized — valuation shifted with politics'],
    essence:'Set murdered his brother Osiris out of envy — the cosmic fratricide that mirrors every usurpation story. He dismembered the body and scattered the pieces across Egypt. Yet Set is not simply evil — he is the god who battles Apophis, the chaos serpent, every night to protect Ra. This ambivalence is key: Tier 3 figures are structurally displaced — functioning where they should not.',
