    function_tags:['usurper','pride','ascent-attempt','god-of-this-world','fallen-angel'],
    etymology:'Latin Lucifer — "light-bearer, morning star." Applied to Isaiah 14:12. Originally a Babylonian king; later applied to a cosmic figure.',
    primary_sources:['Isaiah 14:12-15 (original context: king of Babylon)','2 Corinthians 4:4 ("the god of this age/world")','Ephesians 2:2 ("the prince of the power of the air")','Revelation 12:9 (the dragon cast down)'],
    key_claims:['Isaiah 14:12-15: "I will ascend above the heights of the clouds; I will make myself like the Most High"','The structural sin: claiming to be the Most High while being subordinate — identical to Yaldabaoth\'s','"God of this world" (2 Cor 4:4): Paul acknowledges a lower deity who blinds the minds of unbelievers','Gnostic reading: this "god of this world" IS the Demiurge — the same figure, different tradition\'s name','Orthodox reading: a fallen angel who corrupted what God made good'],
    essence:'Lucifer as the cosmic usurper — the being who declared "I will make myself like the Most High" and fell in the attempt. The structural parallel to Yaldabaoth is exact: both claim to be the highest; both are wrong; both create a domain of limitation. Paul calls him "the god of this world" — acknowledging a lower deity who governs the material realm.',
