    function_tags:['punisher','flood-sender','jealous-of-humanity','wind','storm'],
    etymology:'Sumerian En (lord) + Lil (wind/air/storm). Lord of the wind and storms.',
    primary_sources:['Atrahasis Epic (c.1700 BCE)','Sumerian Flood Story','Myth of Enlil and Ninlil'],
    key_claims:['Decrees the Flood to destroy humanity because they are too noisy','Consistently acts against humanity\'s survival and flourishing','Enki (Cross-Tier) must secretly subvert Enlil\'s decrees to save humans','The contrast Enki/Enlil is the oldest documented good-god vs. punishing-god narrative','Enlil\'s number is 50 — second only to Anu\'s 60'],
    essence:'Enlil is the god who finds humanity too noisy and decrees their destruction. Not once — repeatedly. The Atrahasis Epic shows the gods using plague, drought, and flood in sequence to reduce the human population. It is Enki who always finds the gap in Enlil\'s law and warns the survivors. The Enlil/Enki dynamic is the oldest documented expression of the Demiurge/Liberator relationship.',
