    alternate_names:['Wennefer','Lord of the Dead','The Resurrected King','The First of the Westerners'],
    tradition:'egyptian',
    function_tags:['dying-god','resurrected','death-and-return','underworld-king','divine-spark-model'],
    etymology:'Egyptian Wsir — meaning debated. Possibly "seat of the eye." Greek Osiris.',
    primary_sources:['Pyramid Texts (c.2400 BCE — oldest documentation)','Plutarch, De Iside et Osiride (c.100 CE)','Book of the Dead'],
    key_claims:['Murdered, dismembered into 14 pieces, and resurrected — the archetypal dying and rising god','His resurrection establishes the possibility of human resurrection — every dead Egyptian aspires to become Osiris','Rules the underworld not as punisher but as witness: the heart weighed against his feather','The Osiris myth predates the Christian narrative by 2,400 years in writing','Cross-tier: he descends (through death) and returns — modeling the path for all souls'],
    essence:'Osiris is the model. His death and return is not a one-time historical event but the cosmic template for every soul\'s journey. He was killed, scattered, reassembled by love, and resurrected — not to the same material life but to the divine kingship of the dead. Every pharaoh becomes Osiris at death. Every human who passes the judgment "becomes Osiris."',
    parallel_node_ids:['christ-aeon','tammuz-mediator','dionysus-mediator','balder'],
    cross_tier_relationships:[{node:'set-usurper',rel:'Set\'s murder paradoxically creates the Cross-Tier redeemer'}],
