    tradition:'buddhist',
    function_tags:['compassion','delayed-nirvana','liberator','vow-of-return','mediator'],
    etymology:'Sanskrit: Bodhi (awakening) + sattva (being) = awakening being. Avalokiteshvara: "the lord who gazes down."',
    primary_sources:['Lotus Sutra (c.100 CE)','Prajnaparamita texts','Karandavyuha Sutra (dedicated to Avalokiteshvara)'],
    key_claims:['A Bodhisattva is a being capable of nirvana who voluntarily delays entry to help all beings','Avalokiteshvara\'s vow: "I will not enter nirvana until every last being has been liberated"','Can take 1,000 forms to appear to whoever needs help','The Bodhisattva is at the edge of the ultimate (Tier 1) but choosing to remain in the material realm','Prajnaparamita (perfect wisdom) is the feminine parallel — mother of all Buddhas'],
    essence:'The Bodhisattva is the one who could leave and chose to stay. On the threshold of final nirvana, the Bodhisattva turns around. "Not yet. Not until every last being is free." Avalokiteshvara is said to have wept at the sight of all suffering, and from his tears arose Tara (the Green Savioress). He holds the lotus of enlightenment in the mud of samsara.',
